\section{An Archive Is a Set of Candidates}

An object named directly on the link line participates whether or not someone currently needs one of its definitions. A static archive normally participates selectively. Its index maps exported names to the positions of members that can define them; an index entry is not a definition in the link until the corresponding object is actually admitted. Levine describes both the older \verb|__.SYMDEF| directory and the symbol directory used for COFF and ELF-style Unix archives, then emphasizes that admission must register an entire object's symbols. A member can introduce new requirements as readily as it satisfies an old one \cite[chapter~6]{levine2000linkers}. Today \verb|ar rcs| creates or updates a normal indexed archive, and \verb|nm -s| can display its index.

Distinguish choosing a library file from choosing a member in that file. \verb|-L| lists search directories, and \verb|-lpaint| asks the linker to find a suitable library file under its naming and search rules. With GNU ELF linking, a shared \texttt{libpaint.so} is normally preferred over \texttt{libpaint.a} within a search directory in dynamic search mode. Using explicit \texttt{.a} paths isolates member selection from that choice. Archive extraction is not the runtime search for a shared object and is not controlled by options governing shared-object dependencies such as \verb|--as-needed|.

\section{Two Different Boundaries for Repeated Search}

Levine describes older linkers that could even miss a backward dependency \emph{within} an archive, and explains why ordering members with tools such as \texttt{lorder} and \texttt{tsort} was useful. For a normal indexed archive on a GNU \verb|ld.bfd| link line, extraction can satisfy further demands that admitted members introduce within that archive. When the linker moves past the archive, however, a demand appearing later on the command line does not ordinarily reopen it. Repeating the archive or putting several archives within \verb|--start-group| and \verb|--end-group| expands the scope of the search. A group is repeatedly searched until it produces no more extractions, not until the whole link has no undefined symbols.

This command-line policy is not a law of ELF. ELF LLD remembers earlier archive candidates and can satisfy later demands from them. Its \href{https://lld.llvm.org/ELF/warn_backrefs.html}{\texttt{--warn-backrefs} documentation} discusses both failure on GNU linkers and the more subtle possibility that both links succeed but choose different providers. Treat an order that happens to work on one linker as a test result, not as proof that two link commands are equivalent.

