\section{Trace a Library Cycle by Member}

Write four small C files. All symbols except the program entry in this example return or combine local integer constants; no external package is needed.

\begin{verbatim}
/* main.c: the initial demand is paint, not binder. */
int paint(void);
int main(void) { return paint() != 12; }

/* paint.c: extracting this member adds demand for pigment. */
int pigment(void);
int paint(void) { return pigment() + 2; }

/* pigment.c: this is the edge back into the first archive. */
int binder(void);
int pigment(void) { return binder() + 3; }

/* binder.c: a separate member, not admitted with paint.o. */
int binder(void) { return 7; }
\end{verbatim}

Compile all four sources with \verb|gcc -O0 -c|. Then create the indexed archives and try the ordinary one-pass command line:

\begin{verbatim}
ar rcs libpaint.a binder.o paint.o
ar rcs libpigment.a pigment.o
gcc -fuse-ld=bfd -Wl,-Map=paint.map -o paint \
  main.o libpaint.a libpigment.a
\end{verbatim}

Restrict the trace to the three fixture names: \texttt{main.o} first needs \texttt{paint}, so the first archive admits \texttt{paint.o}, introducing a demand for \texttt{pigment}. The second admits \texttt{pigment.o}, leaving \texttt{binder} undefined when the search ends. Swapping the archives does not help: now \texttt{libpigment.a} is searched before there is any need for its member.

\Cref{fig:visual-286} shows which demand crosses back into an archive the GNU link has already passed.

