Add \texttt{libpaint.a} at the end, or enclose both archives in \verb|-Wl,--start-group| and \verb|-Wl,--end-group|. Both corrected links should run with status zero: the chain returns $7+3+2=12$. Compare the map's archive-member inclusion reasons with the trace; the mere presence of \texttt{binder} in the archive index never explained why it appeared in the output. With LLD and the original order, the link should succeed; add \verb|-Wl,--warn-backrefs| to detect the backward edge.

As a control, make one archive with the members in this order: \texttt{binder.o}, \texttt{pigment.o}, \texttt{paint.o}. GNU \texttt{ld} finds all three even though each newly needed member is earlier in the archive than the member that needed it. The corresponding two-archive failure therefore depends on where the linker leaves an archive behind, not on the sort order of definitions within either archive.

\section{What Counts as a Demand?}

An undefined ELF weak symbol by itself does not trigger extraction. Replace \texttt{main.c} with a declaration of \texttt{paint} qualified by \verb|__attribute__((weak))| and \verb|int main(void) { return paint != 0; }|. The grouped libraries alone should not extract \texttt{paint.o}, and the test should return zero. Add \verb|-Wl,-u,paint| to establish a linker demand before the group is processed: now the function is present and the test returns one. Conversely, a normal undefined global can cause extraction of a member whose definition is weak. Weakness of the incoming demand and weakness of a candidate are different facts.

The invariant for this simple archive model is that every ordinary extraction has an eligible unresolved demand at the point of admission, and each successful extraction registers all of that member's definitions and requirements exactly once. Forced admission with \verb|--whole-archive| is a different policy and may introduce duplicate-definition errors; turn it off after the intended archive. Section garbage collection can subsequently remove admitted sections, but it cannot keep a registration record in an archive member that was never admitted. Use the map to diagnose extraction and the section table to diagnose retention.
